---
// Who makes Agent Codeyard, what it does in short, how to take part, and its
// source, in short sentences (#333, #344). The maker's own projects are on
// /examples/; history, the design decisions and the research are on
// /docs/history/, linked once; the possible improvements are the open Issues.
// Old anchors stay: #why, #decisions, #history, #research and #improvements
// lead to the paragraph that links to History and research.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHead from "../components/PageHead.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import { site } from "../../site.config";
import { href, repoFile } from "../lib/links";
---
<Base title="About" description="Who makes Agent Codeyard, how it relates to JIDAI, what it does in short, and how to contribute.">
  <PageHead title="About" lede="Who makes Agent Codeyard, what it does, and how to take part.">
    <ol class="contents">
      <li><a href="#maker">The maker, JIDAI and the project</a></li>
      <li><a href="#limits">What it does, in short</a></li>
      <li><a href="#contribute">Ready to use, open to contributions</a></li>
      <li><a href="#source">Source and licence</a></li>
    </ol>
  </PageHead>

  <Section id="maker" title="The maker, JIDAI and the project">
    <p>Agent Codeyard is an open-source project by Ahmet Taspinar, founder of <a href="https://jidai.nl/">JIDAI</a>. Ahmet maintains it and merges its pull requests. The projects on <a href={href("examples/")}>Projects</a> are the maker's own: FightAnalytics, a platform for gyms; Physics by Construction, a physics course; and this repository, whose own records are in <a href={href("docs/engineering-records/")}>Engineering records</a>.</p>
    <p id="history"><span id="why"></span><span id="decisions"></span><span id="research"></span><span id="improvements"></span>How it began, the design decisions and the research behind it: <a href={href("docs/history/")}>History and research</a>.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="limits" title="What it does, in short">
    <p>Codeyard writes no code; the AI coding tools do. Around them it adds a round of questions, a plan you approve, checks and a review by a different model per change. The merge stays with you. It is no security boundary: the agents run on your machine, with your rights. <a href={href("features/")}>Why Codeyard</a> explains each part; the <a href={href("faq/")}>FAQ</a> answers the first questions.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="contribute" title="Ready to use, open to contributions">
    <p>Agent Codeyard is ready to use. Create a project from the template, or bring the workflow into a repository you already have, and from the first feature on every change is planned with you, built against agreed requirements, reviewed by a second model and verified before you decide.</p>
    <ul class="rules">
      <li><b>Report a problem or an idea</b> as a GitHub Issue, with what you ran and what happened: <a href={`${site.repository}/issues`}>Open Issues</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Propose a change</b> from an Issue, on its own branch.</li>
      <li><b>Report a security problem privately</b>, not in a public Issue.</li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section id="source" title="Source and licence">
    <dl class="facts">
      <dt>maintainer</dt><dd>Ahmet Taspinar, founder of <a href="https://jidai.nl/">JIDAI</a></dd>
      <dt>source</dt><dd><a href={site.repository}>{site.repository.replace(/^https:\/\//, "")}</a></dd>
      <dt>licence</dt><dd>MIT, in <a href={repoFile("LICENSE")}>the licence file</a>. A project created from the template chooses its own licence. The fonts of this site: <a href={href("fonts/OFL.txt")}>Font licence</a>.</dd>
      <dt>contributing</dt><dd><a href={repoFile("CONTRIBUTING.md")}>Contributing guide</a> and <a href={repoFile("AGENTS.md")}>agent instructions</a></dd>
      <dt>security</dt><dd><a href={repoFile("SECURITY.md")}>Security policy</a></dd>
    </dl>
  </Section>
</Base>
